WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.625
In this lesson we will build and maintain the work in a
Gantt plan.

00:00:04.745 --> 00:00:10.470
You will create a task, edit its details, move and resize
work, and represent progress.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.890
We will also add a milestone, organize rows, and change
several tasks together.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:21.535
Finally, we will inspect what happens to dependent work
when a predecessor is deleted.

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:27.555
Work with a disposable practice copy so that the examples
can be repeated and compared.

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:35.217
Use Insert and choose Task to create another activity.

00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:40.287
Give the task a specific outcome: in our example,
acceptance testing.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:44.207
Enter three working days and choose a fixed start date.

00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:49.302
Alternatively, select a predecessor when the work should
follow another task.

00:00:49.422 --> 00:00:54.522
A dependency takes precedence over a fixed start in this
creation dialog.

00:00:54.642 --> 00:00:58.992
After adding the task, check both its bar and its source
statement.

00:00:59.112 --> 00:01:04.312
Naming the work clearly makes later dependency and
resource discussions easier.

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:11.008
Select Design to open its inspector.

00:01:11.128 --> 00:01:17.203
The name is what the reader sees, while the alias in the
source remains a useful stable reference.

00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:23.498
Rename the displayed work to Interaction design and set
its duration to six working days.

00:01:23.618 --> 00:01:27.468
Move focus out of a valid field to apply the change.

00:01:27.588 --> 00:01:30.388
Keep the source visible while you work.

00:01:30.508 --> 00:01:39.933
A label change should not require you to reconstruct every
relationship manually, and a duration change should not be
confused with a simple zoom operation.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:48.658
For an independently dated task, dragging horizontally
changes its start.

00:01:48.778 --> 00:01:57.703
Moving a task that drives other work can also affect
downstream dates, so pay attention to the move policy and
any review offered by the app.

00:01:57.823 --> 00:02:03.023
In this example the dates are explicit, making the
movement easy to inspect.

00:02:03.143 --> 00:02:08.893
Select Design, move it a short distance, and read the
resulting start in the inspector.

00:02:09.013 --> 00:02:13.813
Use Undo if the move does not represent the intended
scheduling decision.

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:20.933
Dragging a task's right edge changes its duration.

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:26.603
That is different from dragging the body to move the
start, and different again from preview zoom.

00:02:26.723 --> 00:02:32.148
Resize Design and then open its inspector to read the
resulting duration.

00:02:32.268 --> 00:02:38.018
Working calendars can make the bar span more calendar days
than the effort number suggests.

00:02:38.138 --> 00:02:43.763
When precision matters, enter the required number directly
after the visual adjustment.

00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:48.533
Confirm the authored statement before moving on to the
next operation.

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:57.058
For this independent-task practice, set the move policy to
Only task.

00:02:57.178 --> 00:03:04.178
Focus Design and use Alt plus Right Arrow to request a one
day move, then inspect its resulting dates.

00:03:04.298 --> 00:03:09.098
Alt plus Shift plus Right Arrow increases its duration by
one day.

00:03:09.218 --> 00:03:14.418
Control plus Down Arrow reorders the focused task in the
authored task list.

00:03:14.538 --> 00:03:19.338
Undo those three diagram operations to return to the
original source.

00:03:19.458 --> 00:03:24.633
Start a horizontal drag, then hold Shift to snap pointer
movement to weeks.

00:03:24.753 --> 00:03:30.328
Read the new task dates in the inspector and check the
current preview after the edit.

00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:40.008
Progress describes how much work is completed; it is not a
replacement for dates or effort.

00:03:40.128 --> 00:03:44.078
Select Build and enter twenty five percent in Progress.

00:03:44.198 --> 00:03:48.198
Leave the field, then inspect the bar and the new source
statement.

00:03:48.318 --> 00:03:55.718
Use an agreed reporting practice when updating
percentages, especially when several people contribute to
one task.

00:03:55.838 --> 00:04:01.638
A progress value by itself does not explain whether the
remaining work is realistic.

00:04:01.758 --> 00:04:06.758
Forecasting and status-date interpretation are taught in a
later lesson.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:14.742
A milestone represents an event rather than a block of
effort.

00:04:14.862 --> 00:04:18.137
The launch marker already follows Build's end.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:23.582
Add another milestone called Readiness review, using a
fixed date in this example.

00:04:23.702 --> 00:04:30.127
Inspect the difference between a fixed milestone and one
tied to a task's calculated start or end.

00:04:30.247 --> 00:04:37.622
The choice expresses a real planning rule: does the review
have an independently committed date, or should it follow
the work?

00:04:37.742 --> 00:04:41.767
Use the source and preview to check which rule you
created.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:50.092
Horizontal dividers organize task rows into readable
sections.

00:04:50.212 --> 00:04:58.062
They are useful for separating discovery, implementation
and release work without pretending that a section is
another task.

00:04:58.182 --> 00:05:02.007
Add a Release preparation divider at the end of the plan.

00:05:02.127 --> 00:05:09.702
Notes provide explanations that do not belong in a task
name, while task links can point to relevant supporting
material.

00:05:09.822 --> 00:05:12.722
Keep these annotations concise.

00:05:12.842 --> 00:05:20.392
Row ordering and same-row display improve presentation,
but dependencies should still carry the actual scheduling
rules.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.275
Task links open supporting material without changing the
schedule.

00:05:28.395 --> 00:05:33.795
Activate the Design reference and review the destination
in the browser confirmation.

00:05:33.915 --> 00:05:38.990
Cancel the first request, then verify that the authored
source is unchanged.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.510
Open the link again and accept the destination.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:48.080
The reference opens in a separate tab while the original
diagram stays available.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:53.000
Return to the diagram and confirm its current preview
before continuing.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:02.550
When the same change applies to several tasks, select them
together and inspect the bulk panel.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:10.045
Here we select Research and Design, then examine the
available fields for shifting dates, completion and
resources.

00:06:10.165 --> 00:06:17.465
Enter twenty five and choose Set completion for both
tasks, then inspect the source to confirm both changes.

00:06:17.585 --> 00:06:24.485
Check which tasks are included before applying it, and use
the shared Undo history to reconsider the operation.

00:06:24.605 --> 00:06:32.355
Copy and Duplicate are useful for reusing structure, but
inspect the generated identifiers and relationships
afterward.

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:41.642
Select Research and Design together and check that the
panel names the intended tasks.

00:06:41.762 --> 00:06:46.462
Choose Copy and inspect the actual clipboard text in this
training preview.

00:06:46.582 --> 00:06:51.332
Copy prepares reusable content without changing the
authored plan.

00:06:51.452 --> 00:06:56.352
Close the clipboard preview, then choose Duplicate for the
selected tasks.

00:06:56.472 --> 00:07:02.472
Inspect the new declarations and aliases in the source,
and check the new tasks in the drawing.

00:07:02.592 --> 00:07:08.942
Use shared Undo to return to the original plan and verify
that the original declarations remain.

00:07:09.062 --> 00:07:14.662
Reusing a selection still requires a check of identifiers,
dates and relationships.

00:07:18.167 --> 00:07:22.142
Copy Research and Design from the selected task panel.

00:07:22.262 --> 00:07:27.712
Open a second Gantt practice tab and keep a small setup
task in that diagram.

00:07:27.832 --> 00:07:36.557
Place the source cursor before the closing delimiter and
paste the copied task statements, then separate the
closing delimiter onto its own line.

00:07:36.677 --> 00:07:43.927
Verify that Research, Design and their dependency render
in the receiving diagram alongside the setup task.

00:07:44.047 --> 00:07:51.522
Switch between the tabs to verify their independent
source, and check aliases before pasting into a populated
plan.

00:07:51.642 --> 00:07:56.892
Copying task statements does not copy a connected project
or its document links.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:06.300
Deleting a task is a structural change, so inspect its
dependents before and after.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.595
First select Build and read its start date.

00:08:09.715 --> 00:08:13.390
Then delete Design in this disposable example.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:17.910
Open Build again and compare its start with the value you
recorded.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:27.005
The app preserves the dependent task's date when removing
the predecessor rather than silently pulling all following
work to the beginning of the plan.

00:08:27.125 --> 00:08:32.650
Inspect the rewritten source, and use Undo if deletion was
not the intended change.

00:08:36.167 --> 00:08:41.767
Choose the only-task movement policy before selecting
Research and Design together.

00:08:41.887 --> 00:08:48.362
Shift the selected work by two days and verify that the
source changes while the preview remains current.

00:08:48.482 --> 00:08:52.307
Enter Sam in the bulk Resource field and choose Assign.

00:08:52.427 --> 00:08:57.427
Confirm that both selected tasks now have Sam in their
source declarations.

00:08:57.547 --> 00:09:05.447
Combined with the completion and copy examples, this
demonstrates one reviewed intention applied to several
selected tasks.

00:09:08.967 --> 00:09:14.067
Inspect Build and retain its resolved start before
deleting a predecessor.

00:09:14.187 --> 00:09:19.512
This diagram uses a compact arrow chain as well as
explicit scheduling rules.

00:09:19.632 --> 00:09:24.382
Delete Design from the inspector and compare the resulting
dates.

00:09:24.502 --> 00:09:32.977
Build keeps its previous date, the tied gate is pinned to
its earlier date, and the chain becomes a direct arrow
from Analysis to Build.

00:09:33.097 --> 00:09:36.272
Review the source and current preview together.

00:09:36.392 --> 00:09:42.242
Use one shared Undo to recover the original tasks, compact
chain and scheduling rules.

00:09:45.767 --> 00:09:50.617
Pause and create an acceptance-testing task lasting three
working days.

00:09:50.737 --> 00:09:58.237
Add a readiness milestone, organize the release work with
a divider, and record twenty five percent progress for
Build.

00:09:58.357 --> 00:10:03.807
Try one move and one resize, inspecting the values after
each operation.

00:10:03.927 --> 00:10:07.202
Then undo those experimental changes.

00:10:07.322 --> 00:10:14.147
Your final plan should retain its meaningful dependencies
and contain the new work and status you intended.

00:10:14.267 --> 00:10:19.417
Compare source and diagram rather than relying on the
general shape of the bars.

00:10:22.933 --> 00:10:29.083
You can now create work, edit its details, record
progress, and organize a readable schedule.

00:10:29.203 --> 00:10:35.153
Moving, resizing and deleting tasks change the authored
plan, so check their consequences.

00:10:35.273 --> 00:10:42.023
Use milestones for events, notes for explanation, and bulk
operations for a clearly defined selection.

00:10:42.143 --> 00:10:48.943
The next lesson concentrates on the rules behind the
dates: dependencies, lags and working calendars.

00:10:49.063 --> 00:10:52.888
Those rules explain why a task begins where it does.
